          "explanation": "BMI 30+ was highly specific (about 90% of those flagged truly had excess fat) but only about 50% sensitive -- it missed around half of people with excess fat. The tempting answer, that it mislabels muscular people, does happen (like our rugby player), but it is the less common error. The bigger issue is false reassurance: many people with 'normal' BMI carry excess, often visceral, fat -- and in older adults BMI tends to underestimate fat, not overestimate it.",
